{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED at exact head 9fc6b2cf78a71733553b5b46f1005d1e2e96ff1c, primary artifact 7ef8a8a05eb560e2addbe42c92a05b3a16adae0d at performance.\n\nMECHANICAL. The head exists, is not in main, merges cleanly into main 44d8b4fa, and the checkout is clean. All six artifacts stand at that exact commit and are live and non-stale, read from the fold rather than from the request: 5af69046 at .github/workflows/performance.yml, d95d0552 at Makefile, e4c7e73e at cmd/gitseq-perf, 1ac8bba8 at internal/perflane, ae3732ac at internal/perfscenario, and 7ef8a8a0 at performance. 28 files, 3619 insertions.\n\nGATES, measured at this head. git diff --check clean. make vet clean. Focused packages under -race: internal/perflane 1.223s, internal/perfscenario 1.362s, cmd/gitseq-perf 1.212s. make test rc=0 across every package, including cmd/gitseq-mcp 60.695s, internal/service 21.693s, internal/workroom 2.505s.\n\nCONTRACT COMPLETENESS. Ten scenarios — startup, cold_status, warm_status, submit_ack, submit_wait, checkpoint_restart, honest_fallback, quiet_long_poll, concurrent_read_write, bounded_soak. Depths span four orders of magnitude, 100 to 100,000. Five projection shapes including artifact_staleness_heavy. Payload buckets 32, 1024, 16384. Concurrency 1, 4, 16. Nineteen metrics.\n\nThe checkpoint cases are the part I would point at as evidence of care rather than coverage. Tails 0, 1, 10, 255, 256 and 1000 straddle the documented 256-event checkpoint cadence, and each is paired with a depth chosen so the tail is exactly that value — 257/0, 258/1, 267/10, 512/255, 513/256, 1257/1000. That is a boundary test, not a sampling of round numbers.\n\nI also checked the contract against itself. percentile_minimums require 20 samples for p95 and 100 for p99, and the repetition counts are set so warm_status and the submit scenarios get 100 while startup and cold_status get 20. Percentile availability is designed rather than incidental.\n\nWHERE I TRIED TO MAKE THE MEASUREMENT LIE, AND FAILED TO.\n\nAlternating sampling is real. AlternatingSampleOrder reverses base and candidate every other round, so first-run effects are counterbalanced rather than merely disclaimed.\n\nCorrectness digests are trusted by verification, not by assertion. run.go re-opens the workspace independently after each sample, takes a fresh snapshot, and compares its digest against the one the measured path produced; a mismatch is a hard error naming both digests. This runs per sample, not once.\n\nUnavailable metrics cannot masquerade as measured zeros. Evidence[T] requires exactly one of Value and UnavailableReason and refuses both or neither, so the distinction is structural rather than conventional.\n\nPercentiles degrade honestly. Summarize marks p95 and p99 unavailable with a stated reason when the sample count is below the contract minimum, and Max unavailable at zero samples.\n\nFresh per-sample writable state is genuine. Materialize gives each sample its own repository with its own refs and config, sharing only the immutable object store through git alternates.\n\nFixture reuse is guarded on identity. The directory is keyed by contract digest, and a reused manifest is cross-checked against generator version, seed, shape and depth, erroring with \"cached fixture does not match its key\".\n\nFIRST-BASELINE HONESTY, which the conditions single out and which this does unusually well. The baseline is marked \"smoke-observed\", not presented as authoritative. It states plainly that one sample can classify response-size hypotheses but cannot claim latency percentiles. Two hypotheses are classified not_yet_measurable even where a number exists — the 4.44 s depth-100 cold status is recorded and then explicitly refused as evidence, because one sample is not a distribution. That is refusing to draw a negative conclusion as well as a positive one, which is the harder half. The soak is reported as 662 of 1000 operations against a 60-second ceiling, and the concurrency observations are called retained workload observations rather than service-level objectives.\n\nTRACE2 AND PATH PRIVACY. Handled by construction rather than by redaction. trace2Event declares only event, sid and t_abs, so Git's argv, paths, repository names and every other Trace2 field are discarded at decode time and never reach the summary, which is three numbers. There is no redaction code because there is nothing to redact — a field never deserialised cannot leak. Bounded too: a 1 MiB line cap with an explicit scanner buffer.\n\nWORKFLOW. permissions: contents: read, least privilege with no write scope. The upload action is pinned by commit SHA rather than by tag. if-no-files-found: error, so an empty evidence directory fails loudly instead of uploading nothing. 30-day retention. Manual dispatch with a tier input defaulting to smoke, plus a weekly schedule. concurrency with cancel-in-progress: false, so a running measurement is not killed mid-sample, which matters for benchmarks in a way it would not for tests.\n\nONE FINDING, WHICH I ASSESSED AND AM NOT BLOCKING ON. exactObjectDigest is computed once, when a fixture is created, and never recomputed or compared. Every later use reads the recorded value or checks it is non-empty, and it is carried into the evidence record as though it describes the repository actually measured. I initially took this for a real gap, since the whole point of the lane is evidence that can be trusted, and it sits oddly beside the correctness digest, which is verified.\n\nWhat changed my mind is the per-sample trusted-cold-digest comparison. A fixture whose objects drifted in any way that changed a measured result would fail that comparison, per sample, with a hard error. The residual is a drift that alters objects without altering any observable result, which is close to vacuous. Add that CI regenerates fixtures each run — they are gitignored and not cached — and that the reuse path already cross-checks the identifying inputs, and the exposure is a local checkout with a corrupted-but-correctly-keyed fixture producing identical results. I would still rather the digest were re-verified on reuse, since it is cheap and would make the record self-consistent, but it is not a reason to withhold approval.\n\nA smaller observation, not a defect. When a scenario observes no snapshot, CorrectnessDigest falls back to TrustedDigest and the comparison becomes trivially true. That is correct — there is nothing to cross-check — and the recorded digest is the trusted one rather than a fabrication. Worth knowing when reading evidence, because the guard only bites for scenarios that produce a snapshot.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Two new packages, internal/perflane for contract, ordering, statistics, digests, evidence shape and Trace2 parsing, and internal/perfscenario for fixture preparation and scenario execution, with cmd/gitseq-perf as the entry point. The measurement layer depends on app and statusview; nothing in the existing layers depends on it. No contract in docs/reference/architecture.md moves — this is additive, and the kernel, application and documentation boundaries are untouched.\n\nSECURITY. The workflow is least-privileged, pins its action by SHA, references no secrets, and bounds retention. The Trace2 path cannot exfiltrate paths or argv by construction. Fixture preparation and sample materialization write only under performance/fixtures and per-sample scratch directories, both gitignored. Profiling output is similarly confined. I found no boundary that this change widens.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. I looked for what could be deleted without weakening the evidence and did not find much. Each perflane file carries one job — contract validation, case expansion and ordering, percentile summarisation, canonical digests, evidence presence, Trace2 — and each has a matching test. The custom PRNG earns its place because fixture generation must be deterministic across platforms and the standard library's generator does not promise that across versions. The one place I would look again later is the split between order.go's case expansion and contract.go's validation, which touch the same shape from two sides; but they are not duplicated today and merging them would trade clarity for line count.\n\nWHAT I DID NOT VERIFY, so silence does not imply otherwise. I did not execute a full standard-tier comparison run — that is hours of wall clock and needs two revisions built — so the end-to-end benchstat path and the profiling artifacts are reviewed by reading rather than by running. I read the workflow rather than observing a scheduled execution. And I cannot settle from code whether the ten scenarios are the right ten for what this project needs to know; I can only say they are complete against the contract as written and that the contract names the dimensions the request asked for.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:7ef8a8a05eb560e2addbe42c92a05b3a16adae0d","head":"9fc6b2cf78a71733553b5b46f1005d1e2e96ff1c","verdict":"approved"}}